    pearls: "The eczema treatment ladder: emollients (daily) → topical steroids + calcineurin inhibitors (for flares) → phototherapy → systemic immunomodulators. Emollients are the cornerstone of management — they reduce steroid requirements and prevent flares. There is NO role for antihistamines in eczema management except for sleep disturbance from itch (they do not reduce histamine-mediated itch in eczema). Secondary staphylococcal infection is common — treat promptly with anti-staphylococcal antibiotics. Topical steroids should be used: apply to active eczema, use the weakest effective potency, stop when clear, restart when flares.",
    references: ["NICE Guidelines: Atopic Eczema", "AAAAI Guidelines: Atopic Dermatitis", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Dermatology"],
